 ## Overview
-Half-angle slicing is a two-pass per-slice rendering technique for volumetric shadows that orients slice planes perpendicular to the half-angle vector between the eye and light directions, allowing a single slice to be rendered from both viewpoints to compute light attenuation and eye compositing [S1, unofficial][S5, book]. It differs from standard view-aligned or light-aligned slicing by minimizing the number of slices required to resolve lighting details and decoupling light transport computation from data volume resolution, thereby avoiding attenuation leakage artifacts common in 2D shadow buffer approaches [S4, unofficial][S5, book].
+Half-angle slicing is a two-pass per-slice rendering technique for volumetric shadows that orients slice planes perpendicular to the half-angle vector between the eye and light directions [S1, unofficial][S5, book]. This orientation allows a single slice to be rendered from both the observer's and the light's points of view, decoupling light transport computation from data volume resolution [S2][S5, book]. It achieves high-resolution shadow map effects without pre-computation or storage, avoiding the attenuation leakage artifacts common in standard 2D shadow buffers [S2][S5, book].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** Volume scalar field, transfer function (density, color, opacity), camera position/view direction, light direction, bounding box of the volume.
-*   **Outputs:** Final composited image in the eye buffer.
+*   **Inputs:** 3D volume texture, camera position and projection matrix, light direction (and position/projection if point light), bounding box of the volume.
+*   **Outputs:** Final composited image in the eye buffer (framebuffer).
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   **Light Buffer:** Offscreen render target (texture) storing light transmissivity/attenuation. Initialized to $1 - \text{light intensity}$ [S5, book]. Resolution should be 30%–50% higher than the frame buffer to mitigate shadow boundary artifacts [S5, book]. Format: Floating-point (e.g., `R16G16B16A16Sfloat`) to preserve HDR values [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Eye Buffer:** Offscreen render target for accumulating the final image. Format: Same as Light Buffer [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Light Buffer:** An off-screen 2D texture (render target) storing light transmissivity/attenuation. Initialized to $1 - \text{light intensity}$ [S5, book]. Resolution should be 30%–50% higher than the frame buffer to mitigate shadow boundary artifacts [S5, book]. Format: 16-bit floating point per channel (e.g., `R16G16B16A16Sfloat`) to preserve HDR values [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Eye Buffer:** An off-screen 2D texture (or directly the framebuffer) storing the composited color. Format: 16-bit floating point per channel [S1, unofficial].
